WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.380
What's up guys, my name is Colin McNeil from theScore esports

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:07.360
You know there's just something special about a grand final on LAN.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:12.580
There's energy in the air, the stakes are high, the crowd is loud.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:16.320
It's the place where CS:GO legends, are born.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:17.640
Sometimes.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:22.840
It's also the place where rosters can collapse, where dynasties go to die.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.520
Where hopes and dreams at glory can fade away.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:30.760
With that diverse criteria in mind, here is our list

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:37.140
of the Top 10 most impactful and memorable CS:GO grand finals of all time.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:47.320
Starting things off at number 10 is Fnatic’s return
to glory at IEM Katowice 2018, where they

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.420
managed to win their first premier championship
in two years.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:01:00.980
It took seven hours, five maps, 149 rounds,
three overtimes and two ace clutches from

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:05.060
flusha to beat the star-studded lineup of
FaZe Clan.

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:13.240
[Casting]

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:19.380
But in the end, it was Swedes lifting the
hardware, remembering what it felt like to

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.380
win again.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:36.360
[Casting]

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:42.759
No one predicted this grand final between
underdogs Gambit and the second best CS:GO

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:44.600
team from Brazil, Immortals.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.600
This Grand Final was incredible for so many
reasons.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:54.280
Zeus fulfilled a promise he’d made a year
prior to win a motherf***ing Major, Dosia

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.820
gave us an unforgettable grenade play

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:03.380
[Casting]

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:07.180
and Gambit made history as the first CIS team

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.080
to win a Major.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:25.300
[Casting]

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:28.580
It was DreamHack Montreal 2017.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:33.600
Immortals were forced to forfeit the first
map of their best of three grand final against

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.240
North after three of their players showed
up late to the match.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:42.660
(Casting)

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:48.340
North won what would be the first
and only map of the finals in an anticlimactic

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:54.340
win that robbed viewers of the clash of Counter-Strike
titans they had been promised.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:03:07.700
(Casting)

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:12.300
FNS would take to Twitter and
remarked that the Immortals players were late

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.659
due to partying too hard the night before.

00:03:15.659 --> 00:03:21.180
Whether it was purely in jest or not, one
Immortals player in particular did not take

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.340
the comment lightly.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:29.140
In response to that, was the tweet that shocked
the CSGO community and eventually broke up

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:30.100
Immortals:

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:36.840
FaZe Clan entered the finals of IEM Oakland
2017 as a heavy favourite

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:43.080
Ninjas in Pyjamas had taken the entire tournament
by surprise by making it to the finals, but

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:47.790
nobody expected they’d actually be able to
beat FaZe.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:49.689
Well, they did.

00:03:49.689 --> 00:03:55.799
With some crafty play and a lot of aggression,
it was Get_Right, f0rest, REZ, Xzit and draken

00:03:55.799 --> 00:04:01.939
who defied the odds and put on a grand final
that reminded us why NiP are legends

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:02.960
in Counter-Strike.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:11.100
[Casting]

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:18.840
At DreamHack Leipzig in 2016 Luminosity Gaming
showed that Brazilian CS:GO should be taken

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:24.120
seriously by finishing in second place for
the second consecutive big event.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.840
"They were better than us at some times. Yeah, hard game and well played to them."

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:35.500
Then, at MLG Columbus they solidified that
fact when they crushed NaVi 16-2 in the final

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.680
map of the tournament taking home their
first big title win

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:51.500
[Casting]

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:57.640
For a long time Astralis entered every
Major tournament as one of the favourites

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.190
— but could never quite get the job done.

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:06.100
The first ELEAGUE Major would break that curse
for Astralis when they defeated Virtus.Pro

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:08.740
in spectacular comeback fashion.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:35.340
[Casting]

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:41.580
The grand final of Katowice 2015 saw two
of the biggest juggernauts in esports

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:45.280
go toe-to-toe for a Major championship.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.360
The series was well-fought and NiP pushed
it to a third map

00:05:49.360 --> 00:06:01.020
[Casting]

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:04.920
But they just didn't have what it took to defeat Fnatic on Inferno.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:18.080
[Casting]

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:23.640
Our Number 3 Grand Final harkens back to the
beginning of Luminosity’s legacy, which

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:29.120
actually started in crushing defeat at DreamHack
Open Winter 2015.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:42.100
[Casting]

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:44.680
The defeat at the hands of Fnatic would mark the

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:50.840
the beginning of the Swedish CS juggernaut’s
new run with dennis replacing pronax.

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:52.700
[Casting]

00:06:52.700 --> 00:07:00.260
In 2015 at ESL One Cologne Fnatic put a stamp
on one of the most dominating runs in the

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:02.120
history of CS:GO.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.960
The Swedes defeated EnVyUs in a decisive

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:08.780
two consecutive
maps to win their third Major title.

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:24.380
[Casting]

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:31.320
With this win, that Fnatic roster may have
cemented themselves as the best team in CS:GO

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.320
history.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:39.270
It may have only ended less than two months ago, but this one was an instant classic.

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:44.460
Nowhere near the favorites headed into the
tournament, Cloud9 were set to take on EU

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:49.940
powerhouse FaZe Clan.They came from behind
in the final map of the Grand Finals on the

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:55.870
back of incredible mental resilience and veteran-like
play to push the tournament-favorite Faze

00:07:55.870 --> 00:08:00.180
Clan to double-overtime and eventually make
history.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:09.240
[Casting]

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:12.360
We'll be talking about this one for a long time to come.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:30.200
[Casting]

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:33.320
[Crowd chanting] USA! USA! USA!

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:38.320
Well guys, that was our list. But if you think we missed a CS:GO grand final

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:43.040
That should have been in this top 10, I don't know what to say, go make your own list or something.

